From 66a46515eb516ea7517d4b3429213152e92a46de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:45:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 090/206] feat(shorebird): the pool mapping's owner, and the
 image writer that uses it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two halves of the same hookup.

The mapping table's owner was recon (c)5's open question -- "plainly old
index to new index, but which object owns it is not established". It is
this object. Their ImageWriter::Write reaches it through
[[0x1005214b0] + 0x28], and 0x1005214b0 is the ShorebirdLinker singleton
pointer that linker.cc already records shared() as guarding. Two
dereferences before the index mean the slot holds a pointer to something
whose first word is the array base, which is a vector rather than a bare
array. So gap1_ -- the 8 bytes at +0x28 our header had been carrying as
"type unknown, not accessed by any measured function" -- is
object_pool_mapping_, and InitializeWithObjectPoolLinkInfo now keeps what
LinkObjectPools computes instead of discarding it. The 0x88 static_assert
confirms the slot is exactly filled.

The hook itself walks every non-trampoline Instructions payload before
WriteText serialises it. Placement is forced: the edit is in place, so it
has to happen while the bytes are still the compiler's.

Measured rather than assumed, because "it built" and "no complaints" are
the same output as "it never ran". Instrumented temporarily:

  with link data     187 payloads + 1589 payloads, 2324 sites detected
  without link data  no mapping, hook inert

So the self-check path -- re-assemble an access whose index did not move
and require it byte for byte -- ran on 2324 real sites and passed. That
is their free oracle, comparing this decoder and these emitters against
code the same emitters produced during compilation, and it is the
strongest evidence object_pool_editor.cc has had.

⚠️ The rewrite path has zero production coverage. nonidentity=0: on this
fixture base and patch are near-identical programs, so all 1421 entries
map to themselves. Unit tests cover the rewrite; nothing here does.

The first negative control was inert and worth recording. Inverting
AssembleReplacement's kind-1 branch changed nothing, because for
ldr xN,[PP,#imm] both arms emit the same instruction -- LoadFromOffset
and LoadWordFromPoolIndex agree on the common case. That is the same
identity-transformation trap as the backfill cursor. Assembling the
self-check against op.index + 1 instead does move the bytes, and then
"Expected:" / "Actual:" fire and ok goes false on both images.

verify_all.sh is 7 ran / 0 failed / 137 passed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

